<article class="env-lemma" id="05WH">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05WH">Lemma <span data-tag="05WH">110.34.1</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $I \subset R$ be an ideal generated by a countable collection of idempotents. Then $I$ is projective as an $R$-module. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Say $I = (e_1, e_2, e_3, \ldots )$ with $e_ n$ an idempotent of $R$. After inductively replacing $e_{n + 1}$ by $e_ n + (1 - e_ n)e_{n + 1}$ we may assume that $(e_1) \subset (e_2) \subset (e_3) \subset \ldots $ and hence $I = \bigcup _{n \geq 1} (e_ n) = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _ n e_ nR$. In this case 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(I, M) = \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(\mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _ n e_ nR, M) = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ n \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(e_ nR, M) = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ n e_ nM  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Note that the transition maps $e_{n + 1}M \to e_ nM$ are given by multiplication by $e_ n$ and are surjective. Hence by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0598" data-tag="0598">10.86.4</a> the functor $\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(I, M)$ is exact, i.e., $I$ is a projective $R$-module. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>